So I spent some more time playing with this. And I'm starting to think there is something wrong with using 'filter' inside a reactive expression with shiny.

Basically I took the exact same code from above and just changed from using 'filter' to using 'slice'. I changed almost nothing else* and now I get a working graph that updates with a change in the pull down.

* I say 'nothing' but I made a really poor attempt to have the pulldown give two ranges (1:1690 and 1691:3380) but all it did was populate the pulldown with single numbers (1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, etc...) but really that's not too important. So I only slice the data.frame by 1 row at a time. As long as you keep the axes fixed (that's the other stuff I added) you get watch the one dot move every time you change the pull down. I selected the start value of 316 because there is a sizable move from 316 to 317 (so no need to hunt around to see a large move).

But yeah, almost the exact same code; 'slice' works but I can't for the life of me to get 'filter' to work.

library(shiny)
library(ggvis)
library(dplyr)

a <- 1:1690
b <- 1691:nrow(cocaine)

shinyApp(

 ui= fluidPage(

  titlePanel("Cocaine by State"),

 sidebarLayout(

    sidebarPanel(

 uiOutput("choose_state")

    ),

    mainPanel(

     ggvisOutput("ggvis")

    )
  )
),

 server = function(input, output) {

 output$choose_state <- renderUI({

    selectInput("set", "Choose State", c(a,b), selected=316)

 })

 cur_state <- reactive({

 cocaine %>% slice(as.numeric(input$set))

 })

 cur_state %>% ggvis(~potency, ~weight) %>% layer_points() %>% scale_numeric("x", domain = c(0, 100), nice = FALSE) %>% scale_numeric("y", domain = c(0, 505), nice = FALSE) %>% bind_shiny("ggvis")

 }
)

